WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.690
Speaker 1: So we have an opportunity here
to do a little bit of of learning of education

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:09.860
about how our electoral system works and some
of the nuances and particulars that some of

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.190
us may not be familiar with.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:20.470
The story about Mike Pence's possible role
in the 2020 election is again just front and

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:26.439
center, as Donald Trump's new spokeswoman,
Liz Harrington, posted to Twitter.

00:00:26.439 --> 00:00:33.370
A statement from Trump going after Mike Pence
saying Pence could have turned over the election

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.180
if he wanted and essentially given it to Trump.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:42.650
The statement put out yesterday from Trump
reads If the Vice President, Mike Pence had

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:49.280
quote, absolutely no right to change the presidential
election results in the Senate despite fraud

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:55.039
and many other irregularities, how come the
Democrats and rhino Republicans like wacky

00:00:55.039 --> 00:01:01.720
Susan Collins are desperately trying to pass
legislation that will not allow the vice president

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.040
to change the results of the election?

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:09.689
Actually, what they are saying is that Mike
Pence did have the right to change the outcome,

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:12.560
and they now want to take that right away.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.999
Unfortunately, he didn't exercise that power.

00:01:15.999 --> 00:01:20.090
He could have overturned the election.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:21.380
Now.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:28.119
It's not true that the laws that are being
proposed about the vice president's role are

00:01:28.119 --> 00:01:30.990
because the vice president could overturn
the election.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:36.749
It's almost like there's so many ignorant
people that falsely think the vice president

00:01:36.749 --> 00:01:43.320
can just decide who wins that they're looking
at putting something more basic in writing.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:48.579
To that end, but we actually have an infrastructure
of law already that makes it very clear that

00:01:48.579 --> 00:01:53.719
the vice president's role with the Electoral
College is just opening the envelopes literally

00:01:53.719 --> 00:01:55.649
and figuratively, and we're going to get to
that.

00:01:55.649 --> 00:01:56.649
But think about what they're saying.

00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:00.599
I mean, if if what Trump is saying is correct,
it means Kamala Harris could choose the winner

00:02:00.599 --> 00:02:06.469
of the 2024 election if she doesn't like who
the person that supposedly won Kamala could

00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:08.110
say, nope, somebody else won.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.819
And of course, that's not the case.

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:17.140
The truth is that the vice president does
not have the power to do any of the things

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:22.420
that Donald Trump has hoped that Pence would
have done, including, you know, seating an

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:26.650
alternate slate of electors from certain states
or any such thing.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:31.280
The 12th Amendment governs this really, really
clearly.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.269
And you can just Google 12th Amendment and
read the entire thing.

00:02:35.269 --> 00:02:36.629
It's not very long.

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:42.480
But the the important relevant points here
are that the 12th Amendment says the electors

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:49.640
shall meet in their respective states and
vote by ballot for president and vice president.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:54.850
They shall name in their ballots the person
voted for as president.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:56.040
OK, fair.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.659
The president of the Senate, meaning the vice.

00:02:58.659 --> 00:03:03.260
Remember, the VP of the U.S. is the president
of the Senate.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:09.489
The president of the Senate shall in the presence
of the Senate and House, meaning a joint session

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:14.129
open all the certificates and the votes shall
then be counted.

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:20.239
That says nothing about evaluate whether this
is the correct slate of electors.

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:26.109
It's the VP shall open the certificates and
the votes shall then be counted.

00:03:26.109 --> 00:03:28.840
Doesn't even say the vice president will be
doing the counting.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.709
The votes shall be counted.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:35.079
The person having the greatest number of votes
for president shall be the president.

00:03:35.079 --> 00:03:36.269
That's it.

00:03:36.269 --> 00:03:41.459
You then have, in addition to the 12th Amendment,
the Electoral Count Act of 1887.

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:42.849
Now why in 1887?

00:03:42.849 --> 00:03:49.579
Well, in 1876 there was a disputed election
and in the roughly nine, 10 or 11 years that

00:03:49.579 --> 00:03:54.810
followed the Electoral Count Act of 1887 was
put together.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:04:01.319
It also is very clear it no one puts the onus
of resolving disputes to the states.

00:04:01.319 --> 00:04:06.200
If there are disputes, the state resolves
the disputes, not the federal government or

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:07.200
the Congress.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:14.900
So, for example, if a governor from Arizona
certified two different slates of electors,

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:19.859
they sign off on some Democratic electors
and then a slate of Republican electors.

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:22.540
That would be a real problem.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:29.780
If a state just doesn't certify any electors,
that would be a problem that would come to

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:30.780
Congress.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.569
But 90 percent of that is states handle these
issues.

00:04:35.569 --> 00:04:40.080
Now, if you have one of these extenuating
circumstances where the federal government

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:48.040
gets involved, there's a few things Congress
can do if there is a ministerial error, if

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:52.889
there is an elector sent who's not eligible
to be an elector for some reason.

00:04:52.889 --> 00:05:01.210
If there is a candidate certified who isn't
eligible to be president, those would be reasons

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.889
why Congress can get involved.

00:05:03.889 --> 00:05:11.460
It's very clearly very limited, and to deviate
from that requires powers that the vice president

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.460
doesn't have.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:16.009
It depends on the House and Senate, and that's
where all of this stuff comes from.

00:05:16.009 --> 00:05:20.199
A member of the House would stand an object,
and then they need a member of the Senate

00:05:20.199 --> 00:05:21.960
to stand and object with them, etc.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:27.350
So the story that they're telling isn't it's
not only a dangerous story, it's also an untrue

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:28.350
story.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:32.940
And what we're up against is that in March,
Trump and Pence are both speaking back to

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:38.770
back nights at a Republican committee event
in New New Orleans, Louisiana, and it'll be

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:41.910
very interesting to see if they're Trump.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:46.510
Also slams Pence and what the crowd reaction
would be is something I would want to see

00:05:46.510 --> 00:06:56.879
as well.

